        "title": "kernel: tcp: restrict SO_ATTACH_FILTER to priv users"
      },
      {
        "cve": "CVE-2026-53388",
        "cwe": {
          "id": "CWE-416",
          "name": "Use After Free"
        },
        "discovery_date": "2026-07-19T00:00:00+00:00",
        "ids": [
          {
            "system_name": "Red Hat Bugzilla ID",
            "text": "2502255"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "A flaw was found in the Linux kernel's Filesystem in Userspace (FUSE) component. The `fuse_try_move_folio()` function, responsible for managing page cache folios, fails to re-lock a request after initially unlocking it on a successful path. This oversight allows another function, `fuse_chan_abort()`, to prematurely free critical input/output arguments (`fuse_io_args`). Consequently, subsequent operations attempting to access these freed arguments can lead to a use-after-free vulnerability, potentially allowing an attacker to cause a system crash or execute arbitrary code.",
            "title": "Vulnerability description"
          },
          {
            "category": "summary",
            "text": "kernel: fuse: re-lock request before replacing page cache folio",
            "title": "Vulnerability summary"
          },
          {
            "category": "other",
            "text": "This vulnerability is rated as having Moderate impact for Red Hat Enterprise Linux because exploitation requires local access and relies on winning a race condition during concurrent abort operations on user-space filesystem requests. An unprivileged local user must have permissions to interact with or mount a FUSE filesystem to initiate the conflicting input/output transactions. Successful exploitation could destabilize kernel memory, resulting in a system crash or potential privilege escalation.",
            "title": "Statement"
          },
          {
            "category": "general",
            "text": "The CVSS score(s) listed for this vulnerability do not reflect the associated product's status, and are included for informational purposes to better understand the severity of this vulnerability.",
            "title": "CVSS score applicability"
          }
        ],
