        "title": "kernel: net/handshake: Drain pending requests at net namespace exit"
      },
      {
        "cve": "CVE-2026-63979",
        "cwe": {
          "id": "CWE-416",
          "name": "Use After Free"
        },
        "discovery_date": "2026-07-19T00:00:00+00:00",
        "ids": [
          {
            "system_name": "Red Hat Bugzilla ID",
            "text": "2502376"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "A flaw was found in the Linux kernel's `net/handshake` component. A race condition exists where a network handshake request can be removed and its associated file reference released concurrently while the accept-side code attempts to access it. This can lead to the system attempting to read from freed memory or a null pointer, potentially causing system instability or a denial of service.",
            "title": "Vulnerability description"
          },
          {
            "category": "summary",
            "text": "kernel: net/handshake: hand off the pinned file reference to accept_doit",
            "title": "Vulnerability summary"
          },
          {
            "category": "other",
            "text": "This vulnerability is evaluated as a Moderate impact because successfully triggering the underlying race condition requires precise race timing during handshake cancellations, limiting exposure primarily to a local denial of service. The affected kernel handshake subsystem is engaged when kernel-layer TLS facilities (such as RPC-with-TLS) handle connection negotiation with user-space daemons. Standard enterprise deployments that do not actively utilize in-kernel transport encryption handshakes will not invoke the affected code paths.",
            "title": "Statement"
          },
          {
            "category": "general",
            "text": "The CVSS score(s) listed for this vulnerability do not reflect the associated product's status, and are included for informational purposes to better understand the severity of this vulnerability.",
            "title": "CVSS score applicability"
          }
        ],
