          {
            "category": "workaround",
            "details": "Mount SMB/CIFS shares with the `nosuid` mount option to prevent setuid and setgid binaries from executing with elevated permissions, or prevent the `cifs` module from loading if network SMB shares are not needed.\n\n1. Apply `nosuid` to an active mount point:\n```\nmount -o remount,nosuid /path/to/cifs/mount\n```\n\n2. Persist this setting by ensuring `nosuid` is present in the mount options within `/etc/fstab`:\n```\n//server/share /mnt/cifs cifs credentials=/etc/samba/creds,nosuid 0 0\n```\n\n3. Alternatively, if CIFS mounts are not required on the system, disable autoloading of the kernel module:\n```\necho \"install cifs /bin/true\" > /etc/modprobe.d/cifs.conf\n```\n\nCaveats:\nEnforcing `nosuid` prevents any executable located on the mount point from running with elevated setuid or setgid privileges. Blacklisting the `cifs` module prevents all SMB/CIFS network file systems from mounting. Remounting filesystems can disrupt ongoing read and write operations.",
