        "title": "kernel: smb: client: clear setuid/setgid bit on write with cifsacl/modefromsid/posix extensions"
      },
      {
        "cve": "CVE-2026-89777",
        "cwe": {
          "id": "CWE-825",
          "name": "Expired Pointer Dereference"
        },
        "discovery_date": "2026-09-16T00:00:00+00:00",
        "ids": [
          {
            "system_name": "Red Hat Bugzilla ID",
            "text": "2535066"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "A flaw was found in the Linux kernel's vfio/pci component. During device initialization, a memory pointer is not properly cleared after being freed, leading to a use-after-free and double-free vulnerability. A local attacker could exploit this flaw to cause a system crash, resulting in a denial of service, or potentially execute arbitrary code with elevated privileges.",
            "title": "Vulnerability description"
          },
          {
            "category": "summary",
            "text": "kernel: vfio/pci: clear vdev->msi_perm after freeing it on init failure",
            "title": "Vulnerability summary"
          },
          {
            "category": "other",
            "text": "A use-after-free and double-free flaw in the Linux kernel's vfio/pci subsystem has been rated Important by Red Hat. When initialization of the per-device MSI permission table fails (for example, when an underlying allocation fails with -ENOMEM), the error path in vfio_msi_cap_len() frees vdev->msi_perm but leaves the dangling pointer stored in the per-device vfio_pci_core_device structure, which persists across open/close cycles. A local attacker with access to a VFIO PCI device could trigger this failed initialization path and then, on a subsequent open or close of the device, cause the freed object to be reused for MSI config-space accesses (dereferencing and calling the freed perm->readfn/perm->writefn function pointers) or to be freed a second time in vfio_config_free(). This can lead to a kernel panic and system crash (denial of service) or potentially to execution of arbitrary code with elevated privileges. Red Hat rates this flaw as Important with a CVSS 3.1 score of 7.0 (AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H); the higher CVE.org score of 8.8 reflects a lower attack-complexity assessment, whereas Red Hat considers exploitation to depend on reliably steering a memory-allocation failure during device initialization. Within Red Hat's affected product scope, Red Hat Enterprise Linux 6 and 7 (kernel and kernel-rt ELS streams) are not affected because the vulnerable code is not present, while the vulnerable code is present in Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8, 9, and 10 (kernel and kernel-rt), Red Hat In-Vehicle OS, and Red Hat OpenShift versions that ship an affected kernel, and fixes are being pursued through the responsible engineering teams.",
            "title": "Statement"
          },
          {
            "category": "general",
            "text": "The CVSS score(s) listed for this vulnerability do not reflect the associated product's status, and are included for informational purposes to better understand the severity of this vulnerability.",
            "title": "CVSS score applicability"
